Under NC Medicaid, the general eligibility criteria for **CAP/DA services** are that the person must be **enrolled in NC Medicaid** and meet both the policy’s **general coverage standards** and the **CAP/DA-specific criteria**. In general, NC Medicaid covers CAP/DA when the beneficiary:

- is **enrolled in NC Medicaid**; eligibility category restrictions can still make a person ineligible for this service;
- meets **nursing facility level of care (LOC)** requirements for HCBS, as determined by NC Medicaid, and needs long-term care supports typically provided in an institution, related to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
- has a **CAP assessment** showing a reasonable indication that the person will need at least one CAP/DA HCBS service within 30 calendar days of evaluation;
- has a **signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis, or a short-term authorized installation of equipment/modification/technology needed to help the person return to or remain in a private residence and avoid institutional placement;
- can **maintain health, safety, and well-being** in the primary private residence or another approved location with formal and informal supports;
- has paid care needs that can be maintained **within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
- has a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- has an **emergency back-up and disaster plan** identifying who will provide care if usual staff or caregivers are unavailable.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), §§ 2.1.1, 3.2.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current document per amendment check)

In addition, the service must satisfy NC Medicaid’s general medical-necessity-style coverage standards: it must be **individualized and consistent with the beneficiary’s symptoms or diagnosis**, safely furnished, not more than needed, not replace an equally effective and less costly statewide option, and not be provided mainly for the convenience of the beneficiary, caregiver, or provider.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current document per amendment check)

## When CAP/DA is not covered
The policy also says CAP/DA is not covered if the beneficiary does **not** meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0 or the criteria in Section 3.0.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 4.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current document per amendment check)

If you want, I can also turn this into a shorter checklist for screening a client for CAP/DA eligibility.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must be provided under a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the plan period;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the treatment plan. [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)]

Policy 10A also says:

- **All services must be provided according to a treatment plan that meets Subsection 3.2.1.6**; and
- providers must **review and renew or revise treatment plans and goals at least every six calendar months**. [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)]

For documentation, the provider record must include **a copy of the treatment plan**. [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)]

Related federal rehabilitation rules similarly require a **written plan of treatment established before treatment begins** that states the **type, amount, frequency, and duration** of therapy and indicates the **diagnosis and anticipated goals**. [[42 CFR Part 410](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-410)]

**Current source status:** Policy 10A is current with an effective date of **February 1, 2025**. I also checked amendment history for Policy 10A and 42 CFR Part 410, and the amendment tool returned those documents as the authoritative current sources.

Yes. Under the current **CAP/DA policy, Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2**, an eligible beneficiary can transfer, and the transfer must be coordinated so there is **no gap in services**. The controlling rule is **Section 3.3, “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries.”** [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)] (effective Sept. 1, 2025; amendment check shows this is the authoritative current document).

## Governing rule: transfers are allowed
Section **3.3** expressly addresses **“Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries.”** It says the **CME must coordinate the transfer to prevent gaps in service provisions**. Before the transfer, the following steps must be completed:

- determine the anticipated start date of service;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange for a home visit by the receiving entity within **five business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, to assess the home environment and identify health/welfare concerns and mitigation needs; and
- coordinate services so they **start on the first date of the transfer**. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Specific rule for county-to-county CAP/DA transfers
For a **CAP/DA beneficiary transferring to a different county**, Section 3.3 adds that:

- the transferring agency’s CME designee must coordinate with the receiving agency’s CME designee on the agreed transfer date, or **within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both agencies must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record is transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving agency must arrange the **home visit within five business days** of the move; and
- services must begin on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## Supporting context
The policy’s general coverage standards in **Section 3.1** require CAP/DA services to be individualized to the beneficiary’s needs and not furnished primarily for the convenience of the beneficiary, caretaker, or provider. That section does not create the transfer rule itself, but it helps frame how services must continue during and after a transfer. [[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)]

## What I could and could not verify
- I **could verify** that CAP/DA policy allows transfer of eligible beneficiaries and sets detailed coordination requirements in **Section 3.3**.
- I **could not verify from retrieved text** a broader standalone “free choice of provider” rule specifically stating that a beneficiary may switch service providers at will for any reason, because the retrieved “Free Choice of Provider/Freedom of Choice” entries did not return substantive text.
- So the clearest supported answer is: **yes, transfers are permitted, and they are governed by Section 3.3’s coordination, timing, record-transfer, safety-planning, and continuity-of-services requirements.**

If you want, I can also answer the narrower follow-up: **whether this rule applies only to county/CME transfers or also to changing the actual waiver service agency/provider within the same county.**

NC’s CAP/DA waiver is a **home- and community-based services waiver under § 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**, implemented through the federal HCBS waiver rules in **42 C.F.R. Part 441, Subpart G**. In its waiver request, the state must provide CMS with the required **assurances and supporting documentation**, including that waiver participants would otherwise require an institutional level of care, that beneficiary health and welfare will be protected, and that the waiver will be **cost-neutral** compared with institutional care. NC’s CAP/DA policy also reflects that the program operates under required waiver assurances, although the most directly relevant legal requirements come from the federal HCBS waiver regulations rather than NC’s clinical policy. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441); [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Federal governing rule

Although the public-notice rule in **42 C.F.R. § 431.408** governs state notice and comment procedures for certain Medicaid demonstration applications, the substantive authority for a CAP/DA-type HCBS waiver is in **42 C.F.R. Part 441**.

Under **42 C.F.R. § 441.301**, a waiver request must include:

- the **assurances required by § 441.302** and the **supporting documentation required by § 441.303**;
- any requested waivers of statewideness, comparability, or certain community income/resource rules, when applicable;
- a statement about whether the state will refuse HCBS when the cost would exceed the cost of equivalent institutional care;
- confirmation that services will be furnished under a **written person-centered service plan**;
- assurance that services are provided only to beneficiaries who, without them, would require the Medicaid-covered level of care provided in a **hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID**, as applicable;
- a description of the target group and separately defined waiver services. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## What the state must demonstrate in the waiver application

Under **42 C.F.R. § 441.302**, the state must give CMS satisfactory assurances. The key ones include:

- **Health and welfare protections** for beneficiaries;
- **Adequate provider standards**;
- compliance with applicable **state licensure/certification** standards;
- services provided in **home- and community-based settings**;
- an **incident management system** to identify, report, investigate, resolve, track, and trend critical incidents;
- **financial accountability** for waiver funds. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

Under **42 C.F.R. § 441.303**, the state must also provide supporting documentation sufficient to back up those assurances, including:

- safeguards to protect beneficiary **health and welfare**;
- records and information supporting **financial accountability**;
- the state’s plan for **initial evaluations and periodic reevaluations** of need;
- the evaluation form used and how it compares to the form used for institutional placement, if different;
- procedures for keeping written documentation of evaluations and reevaluations;
- a plan for informing beneficiaries of **feasible alternatives** and allowing a choice between institutional services and HCBS;
- an explanation of post-eligibility treatment of income/resources, where applicable;
- an explanation, with supporting documentation satisfactory to CMS, of how the state estimated **average per-capita expenditures**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

Most importantly, **§ 441.303(f)** requires the state to show **cost neutrality**: the estimated annual average per-capita Medicaid cost of waiver services plus other Medicaid services for waiver participants must **not exceed** the estimated annual average per-capita Medicaid cost that would have been incurred without the waiver. That is the core fiscal demonstration CMS requires for a § 1915(c) waiver. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

The same section also requires the state to identify the number of **unduplicated beneficiaries** it intends to serve each year; that number operates as a program cap unless CMS approves a higher number through a waiver amendment. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## NC implementation context

NC’s CAP/DA clinical policy is the current policy document for the program, effective **September 1, 2025**, and it includes sections such as **“Mandated Waiver Assurances”** and other operational requirements. However, in the retrieved text for those CAP/DA sections, the most specific substantive legal requirements about what the state must prove in the waiver application came from the federal regulations, not from the NC policy text itself. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Bottom line

The federal authority for NC’s CAP/DA waiver is **§ 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**, as implemented in **42 C.F.R. Part 441, Subpart G**. In its waiver application, NC must demonstrate at least:

- that CAP/DA participants would otherwise need an institutional level of care;
- that the state has adequate **health, welfare, provider, and financial accountability safeguards**;
- that beneficiaries receive services through **person-centered planning**;
- that the waiver is **cost-neutral** compared with institutional care; and
- that the state has supporting documentation for those assurances, including evaluation, reevaluation, beneficiary choice, and expenditure methodologies. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

If you want, I can also give you the **short answer in citation-ready memo form**, or pull out the **exact federal language on cost neutrality and level-of-care requirements** for use in an appeal or advocacy letter.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to an identified beneficiary need and must support the beneficiary’s health, safety, independence, accessibility, or community integration. The Multidisciplinary Team must document that need and support the request with specific records, including a recommendation, statement of need in the service plan, medical support when applicable, cost information, and related supporting documentation. The current governing policy is **3K-2, effective Sept. 1, 2025**, and I did not find any later amendment replacing it. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA requires the beneficiary to **work collaboratively with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, technology, training and education, and goods and services**. That is the program-level expectation for developing a modification request. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy indicates that equipment, modification, and technology services must be aimed at one or more of these purposes:

- improving, maximizing, or enhancing the beneficiary’s **mobility, safety, or independence**;
- improving **integration into the community**;
- improving the beneficiary’s **environmental and community accessibility**; or
- addressing the beneficiary’s **24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### What kinds of home modifications are contemplated

The policy’s equipment/modification/technology section includes examples of covered home-related modifications, including:

- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- medically necessary emergency egress modifications;
- bathroom accessibility modifications;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- certain floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- door handle and door modifications;
- lifts and related transfer devices;
- certain smart home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan;
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space when removed or damaged during an approved modification. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

### Residence/vehicle setting requirements

The policy says modifications may apply to:

- the beneficiary’s **primary private residence** if owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a **rented residence** when the modifications are portable; or
- a rented residence when the owner allows the modifications. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Documentation the MDT must provide

For equipment, technology, or **home modification** requests, the policy requires documentation that includes:

- **A recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested home modification;
- that recommendation must **state the cost** of the item or modification the beneficiary requires;
- **a statement of need identified in the service plan**;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, **when applicable**;
- **shipping costs itemized** in the proposal request;
- **other information required for the specific item** requested;
- when quotes are required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, **NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required**; and
- in special circumstances, **NC Medicaid or its designee determines which professionals must provide written recommendations**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Additional documentation the case manager/MDT record must include

The policy also describes supporting documentation that must be maintained for modification requests, including:

- a **comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment/modification** and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it; and
- an **invoice from the supplier** showing the date the modification or item was provided and the cost, including related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Training/documentation requirement

If the request involves equipment or technology that requires use training, the policy also requires:

- **a plan for how the beneficiary and family will be trained** on use of the equipment; and
- the training must be **documented by the case manager as completed** and **signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Practical summary

For a CAP/DA home modification request, the MDT should be prepared to show:

1. **Why the modification is needed** for safety, mobility, independence, accessibility, community integration, or round-the-clock support needs;
2. **Where it will be installed** and that the residence/setting qualifies;
3. **What exactly is being requested**;
4. **How much it will cost**, including itemized related costs such as shipping if applicable;
5. **Medical support** for the request, when applicable;
6. **That the need is reflected in the service plan**; and
7. any **required professional recommendations, quotes, training documentation, and supplier invoice**.

