Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet the certification standards in Policy 12B and can be decertified for a defined list of operational, staffing, billing, documentation, and compliance failures.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid’s HIV Case Management policy requires the provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers** meeting the policy’s staff qualifications;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s supervisor qualifications;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- show compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- show compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary, operational, and financial records**;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed ownership, name, address, phone, or dissolution changes within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and no later than **five business days after the actual change**; and
- achieve **national accreditation within one year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.1 (effective 2020-01-15; no amendment returned)

The recertification checklist adds practical application requirements, including that the submission address:
- one physical location,
- signed application materials,
- case managers and supervisors with dates of employment,
- counties served,
- services provided,
- operating hours and emergency after-hours plan,
- accrediting agency name and dates or an explanation,
- satisfaction survey frequency and use of results, and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (current; no amendment returned)

For **recertification**, the provider must:
- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of items from initial certification that changed;
- submit all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit annual quality improvement summaries with outcome metrics; and
- undergo a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of records and business documentation as needed. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.4 (effective 2020-01-15; no amendment returned)

## When a provider can be decertified

A provider may be decertified for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide core service components;
- fraudulent billing practices;
- owner conviction of a felony;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a written **plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- falsification of records;
- violation of beneficiary confidentiality;
- employing staff who do not meet policy qualifications;
- staff failure to complete mandatory basic training within **90 days of employment**;
- staff failure to obtain required CEUs;
- failure to provide adequate supervision for case management staff;
- failure to submit required documentation on time;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a quality improvement program;
- failure to notify NC Medicaid/DHHS timely of agency changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in § 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.5 (effective 2020-01-15; no amendment returned)

## Bottom line

In short, NC Medicaid expects HIV case management providers to be properly experienced, properly staffed and supervised, site-enrolled, accredited, quality-monitored, and responsive to Medicaid oversight. Decertification can occur if the provider fails those standards, commits fraud or record falsification, violates confidentiality, uses unqualified staff, misses training/documentation requirements, or otherwise fails to comply with Medicaid program rules.
